# 3. Data Engineering & Streaming Pipelines

## 3.1. External Data Sources

```
┌─────────────────────────────────┐       ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│        football-data.org        │       │       StatsBomb Open Data       │
├─────────────────────────────────┤       ├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ • Competitions & Seasons        │       │ • High-granularity pitch events │
│ • Fixtures, Results & Standings │       │ • (x, y) shot & pass coordinates│
│ • Squad rosters & basic player  │       │ • Lineups, freeze frames & xG   │
│   biographical attributes       │       │ • Pressures, duels & recoveries │
└─────────────────────────────────┘       └─────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 3.2. Provider Abstraction Pattern

To avoid tight coupling to external vendor payloads, all source clients implement a unified abstract base class:

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
from typing import Any, Dict, List

class FootballDataProvider(ABC):
    """Abstract interface decoupling ingestion logic from vendor-specific APIs."""

    @abstractmethod
    async def get_matches(self, competition_id: str, season: str) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    async def get_teams(self, competition_id: str, season: str) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    async def get_events(self, match_id: str) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
        pass
```

---

## 3.3. Dual Ingestion Pipelines (Batch & Incremental)

```
[Initial Batch Load]     Historical Files ──► Ingestion Worker ──► S3 Lake ──► Bulk Stream ──► ClickHouse
[Daily / Incremental]    Scheduler (Cron) ──► Poller ──► Kafka Topic ──► Micro-batch ETL ──► ClickHouse Upsert
```

---

## 3.4. Kafka Streaming Architecture & Topics

| Topic Name | Purpose | Partitioning Key | Retention |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `football.competition.raw` | Unmodified competition JSON | `competition_id` | 30 days |
| `football.season.raw` | Season metadata | `season_id` | 30 days |
| `football.team.raw` | Team roster and details | `team_id` | 30 days |
| `football.player.raw` | Player profiles | `player_id` | 30 days |
| `football.match.raw` | Match details and scores | `match_id` | 30 days |
| `football.event.raw` | Granular pitch events | `match_id` | 14 days |
| `football.match.normalized` | Validated, normalized match records | `match_id` | 7 days |
| `football.event.normalized` | Standardized event coordinates & types | `match_id` | 7 days |
| `football.player_stats.updated`| Aggregated player match performance | `player_id` | 7 days |
| `football.team_stats.updated`  | Aggregated team season statistics | `team_id` | 7 days |
| `football.dlq` | Invalid, unparsable, or corrupt records | `source` | 90 days |

---

## 3.5. Kafka Event Message Schema

Every message published to Kafka conforms to an immutable envelope:

```json
{
  "event_id": "9b1deb4d-3b7d-4bad-9bdd-2b0d7b3dcb6d",
  "event_type": "football.event.raw",
  "source": "statsbomb",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "occurred_at": "2026-09-24T18:45:00Z",
  "ingested_at": "2026-09-24T18:45:05Z",
  "payload": {
    "match_id": 3890251,
    "event_idx": 451,
    "type": "Shot",
    "player_id": 5503,
    "location": [94.2, 38.5]
  }
}
```

---

## 3.6. Idempotency & Deduplication Strategy

To guarantee that duplicate message deliveries never skew metrics:
1. **Deterministic Unique Keys:** Construct a natural primary key for every record:
   $$\text{unique\_id} = \text{MD5}(\text{source} + \text{":"} + \text{entity} + \text{":"} + \text{provider\_id})$$
2. **ClickHouse ReplacingMergeTree:** When deduplicating during background merges, ClickHouse retains the latest record based on `ingested_at`.
3. **Redis Deduplication Filter:** Workers verify key existence for high-frequency streams before emitting downstream events.

---

## 3.7. Raw Data Lake Storage (MinIO / S3)

All raw API responses are persisted immutably prior to normalization:

```text
football-lake/
├── raw/
│   ├── football-data/
│   │   └── year=2026/month=09/day=24/matches_batch_12.json.gz
│   └── statsbomb/
│       └── match_id=3890251/events_raw.json.gz
├── processed/
│   ├── parquet/matches/
│   └── parquet/events/
└── snapshots/
    └── daily_aggregates_20260924.parquet
```

---

## 3.8. Data Quality Framework & Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)

```
Raw Record ──► [Schema Validation] ──► [Business Range Rules] ──► [Deduplication] ──► Normalized DB
                      │                           │
                      └─────────────┬─────────────┘
                                    ▼ (On Failure)
                             [Dead Letter Queue]
                             (Payload + Reason + TS)
```

### Critical Data Assertions:
- **Referential Integrity:** `match_id`, `team_id`, and `player_id` must match dimension registers.
- **Coordinate Boundaries:** Pitch coordinates must satisfy $0 \le x \le 120$ and $0 \le y \le 80$.
- **Statistical Ranges:** $0.0 \le \text{xG} \le 1.0$; $\text{minutes\_played} \ge 0$; $\text{attendance} \ge 0$.

---

## 3.9. Orchestration with Kestra

Daily pipelines run on automated schedules via declarative YAML flows in Kestra:

```
[1. Fetch API] ──► [2. Validate Schemas] ──► [3. Sink to Kafka & S3] ──► [4. Trigger dbt] ──► [5. Refresh Cache]
```

---

# 4. Data Warehousing & Analytical Modeling

## 4.1. Analytical Engine: ClickHouse vs PostgreSQL

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│           CLICKHOUSE (OLAP ENGINE)           │    │          POSTGRESQL (OLTP ENGINE)            │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤    ├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ • Event tables (millions of rows)            │    │ • User authentication & profile records      │
│ • Aggregations over seasons and match sets   │    │ • Bookmarked comparisons & alerts            │
│ • Sub-second analytical radar & trend stats  │    │ • Application feature flags & UI settings    │
│ • Columnar compression & vectorized execution│    │ • ACID transaction state management          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘    └────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 4.2. Star Schema Data Model

```
                         ┌───────────────────────┐
                         │    dim_competition    │
                         └───────────┬───────────┘
                                     │
                                     ▼
                         ┌───────────────────────┐
                         │      dim_season       │
                         └───────────┬───────────┘
                                     │
                 ┌───────────────────┼───────────────────┐
                 │                   │                   │
                 ▼                   ▼                   ▼
       ┌──────────────────┐ ┌──────────────────┐ ┌──────────────────┐
       │     dim_team     │ │    dim_match     │ │    dim_player    │
       └─────────┬────────┘ └────────┬─────────┘ └────────┬─────────┘
                 │                   │                    │
                 │      ┌────────────┴────────────┐       │
                 └─────►│   fact_player_match     │◄──────┘
                        │   fact_match            │
                        │   fact_event            │
                        └─────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 4.3. Dimension Tables Schema

### `dim_competition`
```sql
CREATE TABLE dim_competition (
    competition_id UInt32,
    name String,
    country String,
    type LowCardinality(String),
    updated_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY competition_id;
```

### `dim_season`
```sql
CREATE TABLE dim_season (
    season_id UInt32,
    competition_id UInt32,
    name String,
    start_date Date,
    end_date Date,
    updated_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (competition_id, season_id);
```

### `dim_team`
```sql
CREATE TABLE dim_team (
    team_id UInt32,
    name String,
    country String,
    stadium String,
    logo_url String,
    updated_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY team_id;
```

### `dim_player`
```sql
CREATE TABLE dim_player (
    player_id UInt32,
    name String,
    date_of_birth Nullable(Date),
    nationality String,
    position LowCardinality(String),
    preferred_foot LowCardinality(String),
    updated_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY player_id;
```

### `dim_match`
```sql
CREATE TABLE dim_match (
    match_id UInt64,
    competition_id UInt32,
    season_id UInt32,
    home_team_id UInt32,
    away_team_id UInt32,
    match_date DateTime,
    status LowCardinality(String),
    updated_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (season_id, match_date, match_id);
```

---

## 4.4. Fact Tables Schema

### `fact_match`
```sql
CREATE TABLE fact_match (
    match_id UInt64,
    home_team_id UInt32,
    away_team_id UInt32,
    home_score UInt8,
    away_score UInt8,
    home_xg Float32,
    away_xg Float32,
    attendance UInt32,
    duration UInt16,
    ingested_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(ingested_at)
ORDER BY match_id;
```

### `fact_player_match`
```sql
CREATE TABLE fact_player_match (
    match_id UInt64,
    player_id UInt32,
    team_id UInt32,
    minutes UInt8,
    goals UInt8,
    assists UInt8,
    shots UInt8,
    shots_on_target UInt8,
    passes UInt16,
    key_passes UInt8,
    xg Float32,
    xa Float32,
    tackles UInt8,
    interceptions UInt8,
    duels UInt8,
    pressures UInt16,
    ingested_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(ingested_at)
ORDER BY (player_id, match_id);
```

### `fact_event`
```sql
CREATE TABLE fact_event (
    event_id UUID,
    match_id UInt64,
    player_id UInt32,
    team_id UInt32,
    event_type LowCardinality(String),
    minute UInt8,
    second UInt8,
    x Float32,
    y Float32,
    end_x Nullable(Float32),
    end_y Nullable(Float32),
    outcome LowCardinality(String),
    ingested_at DateTime DEFAULT now()
) ENGINE = MergeTree()
PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(ingested_at)
ORDER BY (match_id, event_type, minute, second);
```

---

## 4.5. Normalized Event Taxonomy

To eliminate terminology differences between providers (e.g., StatsBomb vs Opta), events are standardized into a canonical enum:

```text
PASS          SHOT          CARRY         DUEL          TACKLE
INTERCEPTION  PRESSURE      FOUL          OFFSIDE       SUBSTITUTION
CARD          BALL_RECOVERY CLEARANCE
```

---

## 4.6. Analytical Metrics Specification

### Player Metrics Suite
- **Scoring & Threat:** Goals, Assists, xG (Expected Goals), xA (Expected Assists), Shots, Shots on Target.
- **Normalized Rates:** Goals/90, Assists/90, xG/90, xA/90, Shots/90.
- **Playmaking & Progression:** Key Passes, Pass Completion Rate (%), Progressive Passes, Progressive Carries, Touches.
- **Defensive & Workrate:** Tackles Won, Interceptions, Ground/Aerial Duels Won, Pressures, Minutes Played.

### Team Metrics Suite
- **Offense & Defense:** Goals Scored, Goals Conceded, Total xG, Expected Goals Against (xGA).
- **Control & Possession:** Possession %, Pass Completion %, Progressive Passes.
- **Pressing & Structure:** PPDA (Passes Allowed Per Defensive Action), Set-piece Goals, Fast-break / Counter-attacking Goals.

---

## 4.7. Data Transformations with Python & dbt

- **Python Workers:** Perform heavy string normalization, regex parsing, coordinate system conversions, and raw JSON decoding.
- **dbt Models:** Manage SQL DAG transformations in ClickHouse:
  - `stg_events` ──► Cleaned and deduplicated events.
  - `int_player_matches` ──► Calculated rolling 90-minute metrics.
  - `mart_player_season_stats` ──► Pre-aggregated table for sub-50ms API queries.

---

# 5. Backend API & Platform Services

## 5.1. Architecture: Modular Monolith (FastAPI)

```text
api/app/
├── api/v1/          # HTTP request handlers & routing
├── domain/          # Pure entity models & business interfaces
├── services/        # Orchestration layer (caching, data fetch)
├── repositories/    # Direct ClickHouse & Postgres query implementations
└── schemas/         # Pydantic serialization models
```

> **Design Choice:**  
> Start with a clean **modular monolith**. Microservices add excessive network complexity and deployment overhead at this phase.

---

## 5.2. RESTful API Endpoints

```
COMPETITIONS & SEASONS
  GET  /api/v1/competitions
  GET  /api/v1/competitions/{id}
  GET  /api/v1/seasons
  GET  /api/v1/seasons/{id}

MATCHES
  GET  /api/v1/matches
  GET  /api/v1/matches/{id}

TEAMS
  GET  /api/v1/teams
  GET  /api/v1/teams/{id}
  GET  /api/v1/teams/{id}/stats

PLAYERS
  GET  /api/v1/players
  GET  /api/v1/players/{id}
  GET  /api/v1/players/{id}/stats
  GET  /api/v1/players/{id}/shots
  GET  /api/v1/players/{id}/passing
  GET  /api/v1/players/{id}/heatmap
  GET  /api/v1/players/compare?ids=123,456&season=2025

AI ANALYST
  POST /api/v1/ai/analyze
```

---

## 5.3. Standard API Response Envelopes

### Success Envelope
```json
{
  "data": {
    "player_id": 1024,
    "name": "Kylian Mbappé",
    "season": "2024/2025",
    "metrics": {
      "goals": 24,
      "xg": 21.8,
      "assists": 8,
      "xa": 6.5
    }
  },
  "meta": {
    "execution_time_ms": 14.2,
    "cached": true,
    "version": "v1"
  }
}
```

### Error Envelope
```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "PLAYER_NOT_FOUND",
    "message": "Player with id 99999 was not found in active registries.",
    "details": {}
  }
}
```

---

## 5.4. Multi-Tier Caching Strategy (Redis)

```
┌─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│ Cache Tier                      │ TTL              │ Example Key Pattern         │
├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│ Heavy (Static & Historical)     │ 24 Hours         │ player:1024:overview        │
│                                 │                  │ season:2024:teams           │
├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│ Moderate (Recent Aggregates)    │ 1 – 6 Hours      │ player:1024:2025:stats      │
│                                 │                  │ match:3890251:summary       │
├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│ Light (AI Query Deduplication)  │ 15 – 30 Minutes  │ ai:cache:<sha256_hash>      │
└─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 5.5. Rate Limiting & Platform Security

- **Rate Limits:** General analytics endpoints allow `100 req/min/user`. AI conversational endpoints enforce `20 req/min/user` to protect compute and LLM token budgets.
- **SQL Injection Defense:** All ClickHouse queries strictly use parameterized queries. Arbitrary LLM-generated SQL strings are blocked at the architecture level.
- **Network Boundaries:** Internal databases (ClickHouse, Postgres, Redis) are kept within private VPC networks, with public traffic routed only through HTTPS/TLS endpoints.

---

## 5.6. Authentication Strategy

- **Phase 1 (MVP):** Public analytics exploration. No sign-in required for core statistics, radar charts, and basic AI queries.
- **Phase 2:** Authentication powered by **Auth.js** (NextAuth) for personalized features:
  - Saved player comparison dashboards.
  - Custom alert thresholds.
  - Conversational history persistence for the AI Analyst.

---

# 6. Frontend Experience & Visualizations

## 6.1. Core UX Principles

$$\text{Search} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Analyze} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Compare} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Synthesize (AI)}$$

The platform prioritizes direct analytical insight over endless tabular browsing. Every page delivers interactive visual graphics backed by clean metrics.

---

## 6.2. Route Hierarchy & Page Breakdown

```
/                             # Analytical Landing Page & Global Search
├── /competitions             # Competition overview & standings
│   └── /[id]                 # Competition detail & table
├── /matches                  # Match listings & fixture browser
│   └── /[id]                 # Match center: timeline, lineups, event stats
├── /teams                    # Team directory
│   └── /[id]                 # Team profile, squad, tactical radar, shot map
├── /players                  # Player directory
│   └── /[id]                 # Player profile, radar charts, shot map, AI summary
├── /compare                  # Side-by-side player & team comparison studio
└── /ai                       # Dedicated AI Football Analyst chat workspace
```

