# 8. Reliability, Testing & Observability

## 8.1. Performance & Latency Budgets

| Target Layer | Metric Goal | Strategy |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Standard Analytics API** | $p95 < 500\text{ms}$ | ClickHouse columnar partitioning and index projections. |
| **Cached API Requests** | $p95 < 200\text{ms}$ | Redis in-memory storage. |
| **AI Analyst First Token** | $< 3.0\text{s}$ | Fast tool dispatch and streaming token responses. |

---

## 8.2. Comprehensive Testing Strategy

```
Backend Testing (pytest)
  ├── Unit Tests: Pydantic schemas, coordinate transformers, provider normalization.
  ├── Integration Tests: API routes, PostgreSQL transactions, ClickHouse queries.
  └── Security Tests: Rate limiting, SQL injection rejection, authentication boundaries.

Data Platform Testing
  ├── Schema Assertions: dbt tests (not_null, unique, accepted_values).
  └── Ingestion Tests: Mock Kafka producers/consumers, idempotency replays.

AI Layer Testing
  ├── Tool Call Correctness: Validating parameter extraction on sample queries.
  └── Guardrail Verification: Flagging hallucinated numbers via regression test suites.

Frontend Testing (Playwright)
  └── End-to-End: Search -> Player Profile -> Pitch Shot Map -> Comparison flow.
```

---

## 8.3. Observability & Monitoring Dashboard

Metrics monitored via OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, and Grafana:
- **Pipeline Health:** Kafka consumer group lag, raw ingestion throughput (records/sec), dead letter queue counts.
- **Data Freshness:** Elapsed time since the last successful upstream sync.
- **API Metrics:** HTTP error rates ($4xx$, $5xx$), response time distributions ($p50$, $p95$, $p99$).
- **AI Analytics:** Token usage, tool latency, and fallback rates.

---

## 8.4. CI/CD Deployment Automation

```text
GitHub Actions Workflow
  ├── 1. Code Quality: Ruff (linter/formatter) & TypeScript compile
  ├── 2. Test Execution: pytest suite & dbt test validations
  ├── 3. Artifact Packaging: Build Docker containers for workers
  ├── 4. Staging Deploy: Automated deployment to preview environments
  └── 5. Production Deploy: Vercel (Next.js) + Container Registry (Workers)
```

---

# 9. Project Execution & CV Highlights

## 9.1. Phased Development Milestones

```
M1: Foundation ──► M2: Ingestion ──► M3: Warehouse ──► M4: Backend ──► M5: Frontend ──► M6: AI ──► M7: Production
```

- **M1: Core Foundation:** Monorepo configuration, Docker Compose, ClickHouse, Postgres, Redis, Kafka local setup.
- **M2: Ingestion Engine:** Provider abstraction, football-data.org / StatsBomb connectors, S3 raw lake sink, Kafka producers.
- **M3: Warehouse Modeling:** ClickHouse schemas, dbt staging/marts models, normalization, data quality assertions.
- **M4: Analytics Backend:** FastAPI modular monolith, ClickHouse repositories, Redis cache layer, comparison endpoints.
- **M5: Web Application:** Next.js pages, player profiles, radar comparisons, interactive SVG pitch shot maps.
- **M6: AI Agent:** LangGraph integration, analytical tool bindings, anti-hallucination guardrails, chat interface.
- **M7: Production Readiness:** CI/CD pipelines, Vercel frontend deployment, worker hosting, observability dashboards.

---

## 9.2. Implementation Order Rationale

> [!TIP]
> **Do not start with the AI layer.**  
> An AI analyst is only as reliable as the underlying data layer it queries. Build and validate the data platform and analytical database first, expose them through stable APIs, and integrate the AI layer once data veracity is established.

---

## 9.3. Sprint 1 Execution Plan (Day 1 – Day 7)

```
Day 1: Monorepo scaffolding, Docker Compose (Postgres, ClickHouse, Redis, Redpanda).
Day 2: Database schemas initialized, ClickHouse connection verification.
Day 3: Provider abstraction implemented; fetch competitions & matches from football-data.org.
Day 4: Stream raw fixtures through Kafka; persist JSON payloads into MinIO raw lake.
Day 5: ClickHouse consumer worker; load and normalize matches, teams, and players.
Day 6: FastAPI endpoints: GET /competitions, GET /matches, GET /players.
Day 7: Next.js frontend scaffolding: Homepage, Competition overview, and basic Player card.
```

*By the end of Sprint 1, the pipeline works end-to-end: External API ──► Kafka ──► ClickHouse ──► FastAPI ──► Next.js.*

---

## 9.4. Feature Definition of Done (DoD)

A feature is considered **Done** only when all criteria are satisfied:
- [ ] Source data client implemented and covered with unit tests.
- [ ] Raw JSON payload preserved in S3/MinIO.
- [ ] Kafka event published with standard envelope and versioned schema.
- [ ] ClickHouse dimension/fact table modeled and dbt tests passing.
- [ ] Data quality rules (integrity and boundary checks) active.
- [ ] FastAPI endpoint returns data within the $p95 < 500\text{ms}$ budget.
- [ ] Redis caching applied where appropriate.
- [ ] UI visualizes metrics accurately with responsive design.
- [ ] Error states and empty states handled gracefully.

---

## 9.5. Minimum Viable Product (MVP) Acceptance Criteria

The MVP is complete when a user can execute the following verification flow:
1. Open the web application and search for a football player.
2. View the player's profile and filter stats across available seasons.
3. Inspect interactive pitch shot maps with coordinate-accurate xG values.
4. Select and compare two players side-by-side across key metrics.
5. Review match center details, lineups, and team stats.
6. Ask the AI Analyst a statistical comparison question and receive a hallucination-free answer backed by tool calls.
7. Confirm that the data was processed through the end-to-end pipeline:  
   $$\text{External Source} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Kafka} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{MinIO} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{ClickHouse} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{FastAPI} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Next.js}$$

---

## 9.6. Portfolio & CV Positioning

### Project Summary Tagline
> **Football Analytics Platform** — An event-driven football intelligence platform using Kafka, ClickHouse, Python, and FastAPI to stream, process, and analyze match- and event-level data. Features an analytical star-schema warehouse and an AI-powered conversational analyst built with LangGraph for deterministic, zero-hallucination football insights.

### Ready-to-Use CV Bullet Points
- **Architecture & Streaming:** Designed an event-driven data platform using Apache Kafka, ClickHouse, Python, and FastAPI to ingest and process match- and event-level football datasets.
- **Data Engineering & Lakehouse:** Built robust ELT pipelines incorporating MinIO raw lake storage, schema validation, idempotency controls, and dbt models for historical performance data.
- **High-Performance Analytics:** Implemented ClickHouse star-schema models and vectorized aggregations, exposing analytical endpoints with sub-second response times ($p95 < 500\text{ms}$).
- **Deterministic AI Engineering:** Developed a LangGraph AI analyst leveraging typed tool-calling to interpret analytical data with strict anti-hallucination guardrails, preventing arbitrary SQL execution.
